WEBVTT

00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:04.080
Hi, I'm John Yum.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:10.680
This is Müller-Brockmann's Grid Systems. My bible —
and the foundation of how I design.

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:17.820
My first portfolio. Someone did steal my
MacBook, and all my files with it.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:25.260
I started as a graphic designer, doing editorial
work, like lookbooks for Nike.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:27.840
Marketing campaigns and posters.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:31.540
And detailed print work, like paper
sample books.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:36.080
And for the last decade, I've been
building products at Airbnb.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:41.840
Host Calendar.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:50.440
Airbnb hosts manage pricing, availability, and
bookings all on the calendar.

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:57.920
The old calendar was confusing and limited — and a
major source of support costs.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.000
We started over from scratch.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:10.502
I sketched hundreds of calendars, every
iteration I could think of,

00:01:10.502 --> 00:01:14.900
really trying to understand how to make
something this complex feel simple.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:21.280
We settled on a grid of individual cells, each
with its own nightly price.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:25.860
Tapping a night opens a quick-action panel,
making hosting effortless.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.620
Like blocking or opening nights.

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:32.600
Updating prices.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:38.361
And applying custom settings — like a
15% discount —

00:01:38.361 --> 00:01:40.780
that update instantly on the calendar.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:47.304
And when guests are checking in, every
reservation is right there —

00:01:47.304 --> 00:01:49.700
so you always know who's staying, and when.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:55.577
And simple icons flag the nights that matter —

00:01:55.577 --> 00:02:00.720
a big tennis tournament in your city, Pride week,
or a holiday like Thanksgiving.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:05.089
And those same quick actions reveal the
context behind each night,

00:02:05.089 --> 00:02:07.240
so hosts can price to meet demand.

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:13.790
And to make pricing easier, we
built price tips —

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:16.240
demand-based suggestions a host can
easily apply.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.949
And with one tap, a host can apply
the suggestions,

00:02:22.949 --> 00:02:25.000
boosting their chances of getting booked.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.920
And a list view for users with
accessibility needs.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:38.520
And a year view — for a higher altitude, to see
a whole year of bookings.

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:43.931
And a multi-listing view for our
professional hosts —

00:02:43.931 --> 00:02:46.000
the ones running many properties at once.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:49.920
Without losing any of the power we built
for single hosts.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:56.220
For our Experiences and Services hosts,
an hourly calendar.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:00.980
So hosts can see who they're meeting
next — and when.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.280
With simple navigation between dates.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.500
And intuitive little touches, like
drag-and-drop.

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.444
Always designed with scale in mind,

00:03:19.444 --> 00:03:22.440
so the tools are the same across all
devices and sizes.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:29.800
All of those surfaces took a year and a half
to create and refine.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:33.620
Now, it takes weeks.

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:39.720
This is the Multi-Calendar — built for
professional hosts operating at scale.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:44.180
With AI, I've been able to layer in live data,

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:50.280
build in logic so hosts can update all
their listings in bulk,

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.100
and see their seasonality to make
smarter choices,

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:02.149
with an extended quick-action system to make pricing
and availability updates fast and

00:04:02.149 --> 00:04:02.480
easy,

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:11.140
while keeping navigation fluid and simple,

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:19.120
with quality-of-life improvements, like
automated grouping,

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:27.780
and a powerful, logic-based suggestion system
that automates prices for events and holidays —

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:35.620
removing pain points our users have felt
for years.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:48.300
It's been a joy to build an entire system
from the ground up —

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.460
a single system across many use cases,
dialing in the smallest details

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:56.100
while holding the same rigor of craft
across every surface.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:01.050
Sketch, sketch, sketch.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:09.540
A big part of how I design is sketching — working
through an idea without expectations.

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:14.080
And I want to walk through one of the
prototypes I made years ago.

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:20.933
A fluid way to move through long flows like
checkout. No more page after page —

00:05:20.933 --> 00:05:26.149
just a stack of simple cards: trip details, a
note to your host, the payment.

00:05:26.149 --> 00:05:27.540
Each one right there.

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:33.611
You could skip around, move back and forth, and
check only what you needed —

00:05:33.611 --> 00:05:35.420
without the process ever feeling heavy.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:41.979
I passed this prototype to our design
systems team —

00:05:41.979 --> 00:05:45.992
and this thinking directly shaped how search
works today at Airbnb.

00:05:45.992 --> 00:05:49.276
A heavy, multi-step flow, collapsed
into a single screen,

00:05:49.276 --> 00:05:52.560
so you can make decisions quickly,
and with context.

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:02.260
Transportation.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:08.820
How might we design for the whole trip,
not just the stay?

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:13.840
Our first focus was the obvious one — flights.

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:17.885
But I quickly realized flights were
just the beginning —

00:06:17.885 --> 00:06:20.460
one of many ways to get from A to B.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:26.152
So I started looking at the common elements
that affect any trip —

00:06:26.152 --> 00:06:28.340
how much it costs, and travel time.

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:34.200
And the ability to choose a
destination by its vibe.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:46.920
Like — what popular cities can you fly to right
now, and how much would it cost?

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:49.940
Or discover somewhere unique and unexpected.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:55.660
Or maybe you want to relax, like a
ski or beach town.

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:05.408
Tap into any city to zoom in, and start
building the whole trip:

00:07:05.408 --> 00:07:06.860
flights, homes, and experiences.

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:12.360
With the ability to see nearby airports, and how long
it takes to reach the destination.

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:19.440
Or zoom in even further — where the whole
system still holds.

00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:22.120
You can immediately shop for flights.

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:36.262
Or zoom into a neighborhood, and the UI
switches to inventory mode —

00:07:36.262 --> 00:07:45.560
so you can find a place to stay, or
something to do.

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:48.720
Or for a road trip, the map redraws around
everywhere within a day's reach.

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:56.280
Or hop on a train — and watch the route unfold
across the country, city by city.

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:00.697
So we packaged it up.

00:08:00.697 --> 00:08:02.666
We did our due diligence on feasibility,

00:08:02.666 --> 00:08:04.917
had countless conversations and site
visits with airlines,

00:08:04.917 --> 00:08:09.700
and took it all the way to our CEO. And we
got the green light to go.

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:13.260
This was early February, 2020.

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:16.631
And then Covid hit. Airlines were on the
verge of bankruptcy.

00:08:16.631 --> 00:08:19.360
And Airbnb had to let go of a quarter
of the company.

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:29.320
But the idea never really left me.
